<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Troubles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1968">1968</a>–<a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Northern Ireland conflict · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Troubles were the conflict in Northern Ireland from the late 1960s to 1998 between mainly Catholic nationalists and republicans seeking a united Ireland and mainly Protestant unionists and loyalists, with the British Army and police also involved. Beginning with clashes over civil rights in 1968–1969, it involved bombing and shooting campaigns by the Provisional IRA and loyalist paramilitaries and killed about 3,600 people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> of 1998 largely ended it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-troubles.jpg" alt="The Troubles" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: louise price · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Free_Derry_Corner_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1317804.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The roots of the conflict lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a> of 1609, when Scottish and English settlers were given land taken from the native Irish, and in later conflicts between Catholics and Protestants. As Home Rule drew near in 1912, unionists led by Edward Carson signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulster-covenant">Ulster Covenant</a> and formed the Ulster Volunteer Force. Unionists and loyalists, mostly Ulster Protestants, wanted Northern Ireland to remain in the United Kingdom, while nationalists and republicans, mostly Catholics, wanted a united Ireland. It was not a religious war, though the division ran along those lines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Writers disagree on the start of the Troubles, proposing the formation of the modern Ulster Volunteer Force in 1966, the civil rights march in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> on 5 October 1968, or the deployment of British troops on 14 August 1969. Tensions grew during a campaign by the Northern Ireland Civil Rights Association against discrimination against the Catholic minority. The government tried to suppress the protests, the Royal Ulster Constabulary was criticised for sectarianism and brutality, and loyalists opposed the campaign. The riots of August 1969 brought British troops, in what became the Army's longest operation, and peace walls were built to separate communities.</p><p>Catholic support for the Army fell after Bloody Sunday, 30 January 1972, when British paratroopers shot dead thirteen unarmed men at an anti-internment rally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a>. The violence included bombings and shootings by the Provisional IRA, the INLA and loyalist groups such as the UVF and UDA, as well as killings by state forces, collusion, riots and the creation of no-go areas. In 1981 ten republican prisoners died on hunger strike, and the first, Bobby Sands, had been elected to Parliament. Republican and loyalist ceasefires were declared in 1994, and the IRA ended its own with the Docklands bombing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 9 February 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Talks between the main parties led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> of 1998, which restored self-government to Northern Ireland on the basis of power-sharing and provided for consent, civil and political rights, police reform, paramilitary disarmament and the early release of prisoners. An executive with the four main parties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a> among them, was formed in 1999, and the Royal Ulster Constabulary became the Police Service of Northern Ireland.</p><p>More than 3,500 people died. About 52% were civilians, 32% members of the British security forces and 16% members of paramilitary groups. Republican paramilitaries were responsible for about 60% of deaths, loyalists for 30% and the security forces for 10%.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Troubles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Until 1972 Northern Ireland had its own parliament at Stormont, which was then suspended amid the <b>Troubles</b>, and direct rule from London was imposed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The civil rights movement of the late 1960s was followed by the <b>Troubles</b>, which killed about 3,600 people, and direct rule from London from 1972.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-ireland">Northern Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
